Lazy Initialization: Objects should not be created until they are actually needed to optimize memory and performance. Example: public class Lazy {
    List<String> names;

    public List<String> getNames() {
        if (names == null) {
            names = new ArrayList<>();
        }
        return names;
    }
}

Avoid Wrapper Classes for Primitives: Use primitive types (int, double, etc.) instead of wrapper classes (Integer, Double) when possible, as it reduces memory usage and improves performance. // Inefficient, using Integer wrapper instead of int
void slowMethod() {
    Integer totalValue = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 100000000; i++) {
        totalValue += i;
    }
}

Enums vs. Integer Constants: Enums are preferred over integer constants for fixed sets of constants, as they offer type safety and better organization.  Example: // Inefficient use of integer constants
public static final int WINTER = 0;
public static final int SUMMER = 1;

Enum Advantages:  Example: enum Seasons { SUMMER, WINTER, AUTUMN, SPRING }  Example: public enum Seasons {
    SUMMER("Yellow", 30),
    WINTER("Grey", -5),
    AUTUMN("Orange", 20),
    SPRING("Red", 10);

    private final String colorOfSeason;
    private final int avgTemperature;

    Seasons(String colorOfSeason, int avgTemperature) {
        this.colorOfSeason = colorOfSeason;
        this.avgTemperature = avgTemperature;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return this.colorOfSeason + " " + this.avgTemperature;
    }
}

